  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above normal temperatures and humidity \ncontinues through Tuesday. Relief from the heat & humidity comes\nlate week and especially this weekend. \n\nA bit of a persistence temperature forecast to start the week as the \npattern is overall pretty zonal. Model soundings indicate mixing up \nto near 850 mb where temperatures are/will be hovering around 16C \nresulting in highs in the upper 80s, perhaps touching 90s in the \nwarmest spots today and Tuesday. We'll then see a gradual cooling \ndown through the work week and especially by the weekend. 850 mb \ntemps drop to near 13C by mid week leading to highs in the upper 70s \n(high elevations) to mid 80s. The weekend, though, looks quite \ncomfortable as dewpoints which will be elevated at times through the \nweek but never oppressive, really drop by the weekend into the upper \n40s to low 50s! This leads to the chance for some cooler nights in \nthe 50s while afternoon temps may not make it out of the 70s for \nmany locations. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather continues this week with shower \nand thunderstorms chances returning this evening, as well as \nsome time from late Wednesday night into Thursday night.\n\nLooking at a rather narrow time window for thunderstorms this \nafternoon, pretty much from 5-9 PM. Most-unstable CAPE values peak \nabout 750-1,100 J/kg during this time, then quickly diminish after \nsunset. There is just not enough synoptic forcing to maintain it \nmuch longer. While a few thunderstorms are possible after sunset, \nthey should be weakening as they move across southern New England, \nespecially by the time they reach eastern MA. Anticipating the \nmajority of the stronger convection over PA and NY state to remain \nmainly to our south and west. Will continue to monitor this though \nas it could remain somewhat as potent moving into the CT River \nvalley.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday should remain dry and mostly sunny with more \ncomfortable humidity.\n\nComplicated forecast from late Wednesday night into Thursday night. \nExpecting a frontal boundary to meander across southern New England. \nThus, the timing is not well known. This front should provide a \ntrigger for some showers at times, but it should not be a washout. A \nhigh pressure should finally get close enough on Friday to push this \nfront offshore, and return drier and cooler weather into early next \nweek.",
